\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For an analytic univalent $f$ in the unit disk and $\lambda>0$, set
\[I_{-\lambda}(r,f')=\left(\frac1{2\pi}\int_0^{2\pi}|f'(re^{i\theta})|^{-\lambda}\,d\theta\right)^{1/\lambda}.\]
Determine the maximal growth rate as $r\uparrow1$. In particular, for $\lambda=2$, must it be $O((1-r)^{-1/2})$, the rate of the Koebe function?
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07200}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
For an analytic univalent $f$ in the unit disk and $\lambda>0$, set
\[I_{-\lambda}(r,f')=\left(\frac1{2\pi}\int_0^{2\pi}|f'(re^{i\theta})|^{-\lambda}\,d\theta\right)^{1/\lambda}.\]
Determine the maximal growth rate as $r\uparrow1$. In particular, for $\lambda=2$, must it be $O((1-r)^{-1/2})$, the rate of the Koebe function?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 2306109 (AMR-022-6109). Catalogue attribution under CC BY 4.0.
\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
